  "result": "I graded quiz 0001 and wrote the grades to `grades/0001-s1.json`. You got 2 correct, 1 partial and 1 wrong.\n\n- **q1 (correct):** A tree picks the split that most reduces the squared error in the two children.\n- **q2 (correct):** The leaf's mean is 240 thousand dollars, and your $240,000 is the same amount.\n- **q3 (partial):** \"Overfits and memorizes\" is right, but the answer doesn't say why that hurts new predictions. With one row per leaf, each leaf copies a noisy target. A different training sample would then give a very different tree, which is high variance.\n- **q4 (wrong, M2):** You picked \"continues the upward trend\", but a tree has no slope to extend. A 5,000 sq ft house lands in the same leaf as the largest training houses, so it gets that leaf's mean price. The prediction stays flat.\n\n**Next step:** Sketch a one-feature tree (size against price) and draw its predictions as a step function. Mark where it goes flat past the largest training point. Then explain in one sentence how a deep tree changes with a different sample. That same variance idea is why averaging many trees in a random forest helps.\n\nI also added learning record `0002-trees-no-extrapolation-and-variance-reasoning.md` and updated `NOTES.md`.",
